{"type":"message","id":"1e9c1e3b","parentId":"888ee018","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:08:03.209Z","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"[Sat 2026-05-23 08:07 UTC] [Subagent Context] You are running as a subagent (depth 1/1). Results auto-announce to your requester; do not busy-poll for status.\n\n[Subagent Task]: Generate a feature image for this article. Read /tmp/crypto-run-20260523-080242/research/validated.json (also at /tmp/research.json) to find the article title, topic, and primary crypto asset. Use the Scene Formula in your SOUL.md to pick the right human subject and scene, then run the API calls and logo stamp steps exactly as defined in your SOUL.md."}],"timestamp":1779523683200}}
{"type":"message","id":"31eb9b32","parentId":"1e9c1e3b","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:08:04.870Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_o0jt7ARfneov2hvErjgjsCnc","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/crypto-run-20260523-080242/research/validated.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_YOSoLcTGRsx3AeoOZCkiTy5U","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/tmp/research.json"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_FFv2Tv3EiwCvHhHx3LMR5Pub","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/home/bhard/.openclaw/workspace-creator/SOUL.md"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"local-bifrost","model":"gpt-5.4-mini","usage":{"input":5955,"output":208,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":6163,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1779523683211,"responseId":"chatcmpl-DibgqRO4H2Aw2Rreua623r45MfN6D"}}
{"type":"message","id":"125ecbc0","parentId":"31eb9b32","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:08:04.900Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_o0jt7ARfneov2hvErjgjsCnc","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"congress-polymarket-kalshi-insider-trading-probe-2026-05-23\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US congressional insider-trading probe into prediction markets Polymarket and Kalshi\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"prediction market probe\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Congress opens insider-trading probe into Polymarket and Kalshi over fears government officials could profit from nonpublic information\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Polygon\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"polygon-ecosystem-token\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer said the committee is investigating Polymarket and Kalshi over whether government employees may be using insider or classified information to profit from prediction-market trades tied to policy, elections, wars, and other state actions.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour seeking records on identity verification, geographic restrictions, and how the platforms detect and flag anomalous trading activity.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph said Comer referenced suspiciously timed trades highlighted in a May 13 New York Times report, including bets linked to Israel's military actions against Iran, a Trump ceasefire announcement, and congressional election contracts.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph added that prosecutors previously charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke with using confidential government information to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket contracts tied to the capture of Nicolás Maduro; Van Dyke pleaded not guilty.\",\n    \"CoinDesk cited Bubblemaps CEO Nicolas Vaiman saying his team identified 80 Polymarket bets with a 98% win rate, a pattern he argued is statistically impossible to explain by luck and therefore suggestive of information asymmetry or insider trading.\",\n    \"Decrypt reported that Kalshi launched Americans for Fair Markets, a well-capitalized advocacy group tied to former White House communications aide Taylor Budowich, on the same day the probe became public, underscoring how high the policy stakes have become for prediction markets in Washington.\",\n    \"CoinDesk said Bernstein estimated prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could hit about $240 billion in 2026, and may grow toward $1 trillion by 2030, explaining why lawmakers now see the sector as large enough to create national-security, ethics, and consumer-protection risks.\",\n    \"Across the coverage, both companies said they maintain anti-insider-trading safeguards and will engage with lawmakers, while the probe could lead to legislation restricting or banning participation by members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"SOURCE: CoinDesk\\nURL: https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\\n\\nCoinDesk reported that the U.S. House Oversight Committee is opening a probe into prediction-market platforms Polymarket and Kalshi amid fears that government employees could use insider or classified information to make money on contracts tied to public policy, elections, and geopolitical events. The article said Chair James Comer told CNBC that members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees may have access to information that could generate “huge profits” when traded through prediction markets. CoinDesk said Comer plans not only to investigate how widespread such conduct may already be, but also to build a case for legislation that could bar government officials from participating in these markets entirely.\\n\\nThe piece said Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour requesting internal records and clarity on a set of core compliance controls. Those requests focus on identity verification, enforcement of geographic restrictions, and the detection of anomalous trading activity. CoinDesk framed the probe as part of a broader congressional effort to understand whether these platforms can be manipulated by actors with privileged access to national-security or policy information.\\n\\nCoinDesk also gave important market context. It cited a Bernstein report from April estimating that prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could rise to around $240 billion in 2026, and may approach $1 trillion by 2030 as the category expands from niche wagering into broader “information markets” spanning sports, politics, crypto, and macroeconomic events. The article further quoted Bubblemaps co-founder and CEO Nicolas Vaiman, who warned that visible irregular betting patterns could create national-security concerns if hostile foreign actors can infer privileged knowledge from public market activity. Vaiman said his team found 80 bets on Polymarket with a 98% win rate, arguing that such results are statistically implausible through luck alone.\\n\\nSOURCE: CoinTelegraph\\nURL: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\\n\\nCoinTelegraph confirmed that the investigation centers on allegedly suspiciously timed trades involving government-sensitive events. The outlet said Comer was referring in part to a May 13 New York Times report describing users who bet on Israel’s military actions against Iran, on President Donald Trump announcing a ceasefire in that conflict, and on contracts connected to congressional elections. According to CoinTelegraph, Comer’s public comments suggest lawmakers believe prediction markets may offer a new venue where politically connected or government-linked insiders can monetize nonpublic information faster than in traditional financial markets.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph added legally significant background by pointing to a prior Justice Department case announced in April. Prosecutors charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke over allegations that he used confidential government information related to the operation targeting Nicolás Maduro to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket event contracts. The report said Van Dyke pleaded not guilty and was released on $250,000 bail with travel restrictions. That case matters because it gives Congress a concrete enforcement example rather than a purely theoretical risk.\\n\\nThe article also noted that Polymarket said in March it had updated its approach to potential insider trading, while Kalshi said in April that it had barred three U.S. politicians from betting on their own races. Both firms indicated they would engage with lawmakers and defended their integrity controls. CoinTelegraph’s framing makes clear that the present probe is not happening in isolation: it follows a week of intensifying scrutiny in Washington and escalating concern that event contracts may blur the boundary between financial markets, gambling, and ethics law.\\n\\nSOURCE: Decrypt\\nURL: https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\\n\\nDecrypt approached the story through Kalshi’s policy response. It reported that Kalshi unveiled Americans for Fair Markets, described as a “well-capitalized” advocacy group, the same day Congress opened the insider-trading probe. The group tapped Taylor Budowich, a former White House communications official and pro-Trump PAC leader, as a strategic adviser. Decrypt said the launch is designed to shape how Capitol Hill views prediction markets and to counter attacks from entrenched sportsbook and casino interests.\\n\\nDecrypt stressed that Kalshi is trying to distinguish itself from Polymarket and from traditional betting operators by emphasizing its status as a federally regulated exchange under the CFTC. The article quoted rhetoric from Americans for Fair Markets arguing that banning prediction markets would simply push activity offshore to venues with weaker identity checks, lighter consumer protections, and less enforcement against insider trading. At the same time, Decrypt showed that the gaming lobby is fighting back, with American Gaming Association CEO Bill Miller telling Congress that these platforms are essentially backdoor sports-betting operations wrapped in financial-market language.\\n\\nCOMBINED SYNTHESIS\\n\\nAcross all three reports, the strongest single event is not merely that Congress asked a few questions, but that U.S. lawmakers have escalated prediction markets into a national policy issue touching ethics, market integrity, gambling law, and national security. The probe matters because Polymarket and Kalshi sit at the center of a rapidly expanding sector whose contracts can reflect military action, diplomatic announcements, elections, and government decisions in real time. If officials with privileged access are trading in that environment, the issue is larger than ordinary platform compliance; it becomes a state-integrity problem.\\n\\nThe coverage also reveals why this story has momentum right now. CoinDesk provides scale, showing prediction markets are becoming large enough to matter systemically. CoinTelegraph provides the enforcement trail and the concrete Maduro-related case that makes the risk legible to lawmakers. Decrypt shows the immediate political counteroffensive, with Kalshi moving to build a lobbying apparatus as scrutiny intensifies. Taken together, the reporting suggests Washington is entering a new phase: prediction markets are no longer just an innovation story or a crypto-curiosity story, but a contested regulatory battleground that could soon produce new rules on who may trade, what event contracts are permissible, and how platforms must police insider information.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523684900}}
{"type":"message","id":"a2c4bfbd","parentId":"125ecbc0","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:08:04.900Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_YOSoLcTGRsx3AeoOZCkiTy5U","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"congress-polymarket-kalshi-insider-trading-probe-2026-05-23\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US congressional insider-trading probe into prediction markets Polymarket and Kalshi\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"prediction market probe\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"Congress opens insider-trading probe into Polymarket and Kalshi over fears government officials could profit from nonpublic information\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Polygon\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"polygon-ecosystem-token\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"CoinDesk\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"Decrypt\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\",\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer said the committee is investigating Polymarket and Kalshi over whether government employees may be using insider or classified information to profit from prediction-market trades tied to policy, elections, wars, and other state actions.\",\n    \"CoinDesk reported Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour seeking records on identity verification, geographic restrictions, and how the platforms detect and flag anomalous trading activity.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph said Comer referenced suspiciously timed trades highlighted in a May 13 New York Times report, including bets linked to Israel's military actions against Iran, a Trump ceasefire announcement, and congressional election contracts.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph added that prosecutors previously charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke with using confidential government information to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket contracts tied to the capture of Nicolás Maduro; Van Dyke pleaded not guilty.\",\n    \"CoinDesk cited Bubblemaps CEO Nicolas Vaiman saying his team identified 80 Polymarket bets with a 98% win rate, a pattern he argued is statistically impossible to explain by luck and therefore suggestive of information asymmetry or insider trading.\",\n    \"Decrypt reported that Kalshi launched Americans for Fair Markets, a well-capitalized advocacy group tied to former White House communications aide Taylor Budowich, on the same day the probe became public, underscoring how high the policy stakes have become for prediction markets in Washington.\",\n    \"CoinDesk said Bernstein estimated prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could hit about $240 billion in 2026, and may grow toward $1 trillion by 2030, explaining why lawmakers now see the sector as large enough to create national-security, ethics, and consumer-protection risks.\",\n    \"Across the coverage, both companies said they maintain anti-insider-trading safeguards and will engage with lawmakers, while the probe could lead to legislation restricting or banning participation by members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"SOURCE: CoinDesk\\nURL: https://www.coindesk.com/policy/2026/05/22/congress-probes-polymarket-and-kalshi-over-fears-government-employees-are-trading-on-secret-info\\n\\nCoinDesk reported that the U.S. House Oversight Committee is opening a probe into prediction-market platforms Polymarket and Kalshi amid fears that government employees could use insider or classified information to make money on contracts tied to public policy, elections, and geopolitical events. The article said Chair James Comer told CNBC that members of Congress, administration officials, and other government employees may have access to information that could generate “huge profits” when traded through prediction markets. CoinDesk said Comer plans not only to investigate how widespread such conduct may already be, but also to build a case for legislation that could bar government officials from participating in these markets entirely.\\n\\nThe piece said Comer sent letters to Polymarket CEO Shayne Coplan and Kalshi CEO Tarek Mansour requesting internal records and clarity on a set of core compliance controls. Those requests focus on identity verification, enforcement of geographic restrictions, and the detection of anomalous trading activity. CoinDesk framed the probe as part of a broader congressional effort to understand whether these platforms can be manipulated by actors with privileged access to national-security or policy information.\\n\\nCoinDesk also gave important market context. It cited a Bernstein report from April estimating that prediction-market volumes reached $51 billion last year, could rise to around $240 billion in 2026, and may approach $1 trillion by 2030 as the category expands from niche wagering into broader “information markets” spanning sports, politics, crypto, and macroeconomic events. The article further quoted Bubblemaps co-founder and CEO Nicolas Vaiman, who warned that visible irregular betting patterns could create national-security concerns if hostile foreign actors can infer privileged knowledge from public market activity. Vaiman said his team found 80 bets on Polymarket with a 98% win rate, arguing that such results are statistically implausible through luck alone.\\n\\nSOURCE: CoinTelegraph\\nURL: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-house-probe-kalshi-polymarket-insider-trading?utm_source=rss_feed&utm_medium=rss&utm_campaign=rss_partner_inbound\\n\\nCoinTelegraph confirmed that the investigation centers on allegedly suspiciously timed trades involving government-sensitive events. The outlet said Comer was referring in part to a May 13 New York Times report describing users who bet on Israel’s military actions against Iran, on President Donald Trump announcing a ceasefire in that conflict, and on contracts connected to congressional elections. According to CoinTelegraph, Comer’s public comments suggest lawmakers believe prediction markets may offer a new venue where politically connected or government-linked insiders can monetize nonpublic information faster than in traditional financial markets.\\n\\nCoinTelegraph added legally significant background by pointing to a prior Justice Department case announced in April. Prosecutors charged Master Sergeant Gannon Ken Van Dyke over allegations that he used confidential government information related to the operation targeting Nicolás Maduro to profit by more than $400,000 on Polymarket event contracts. The report said Van Dyke pleaded not guilty and was released on $250,000 bail with travel restrictions. That case matters because it gives Congress a concrete enforcement example rather than a purely theoretical risk.\\n\\nThe article also noted that Polymarket said in March it had updated its approach to potential insider trading, while Kalshi said in April that it had barred three U.S. politicians from betting on their own races. Both firms indicated they would engage with lawmakers and defended their integrity controls. CoinTelegraph’s framing makes clear that the present probe is not happening in isolation: it follows a week of intensifying scrutiny in Washington and escalating concern that event contracts may blur the boundary between financial markets, gambling, and ethics law.\\n\\nSOURCE: Decrypt\\nURL: https://decrypt.co/368803/kalshi-fair-markets-lobby-group-congress-opens-insider-trading-probe\\n\\nDecrypt approached the story through Kalshi’s policy response. It reported that Kalshi unveiled Americans for Fair Markets, described as a “well-capitalized” advocacy group, the same day Congress opened the insider-trading probe. The group tapped Taylor Budowich, a former White House communications official and pro-Trump PAC leader, as a strategic adviser. Decrypt said the launch is designed to shape how Capitol Hill views prediction markets and to counter attacks from entrenched sportsbook and casino interests.\\n\\nDecrypt stressed that Kalshi is trying to distinguish itself from Polymarket and from traditional betting operators by emphasizing its status as a federally regulated exchange under the CFTC. The article quoted rhetoric from Americans for Fair Markets arguing that banning prediction markets would simply push activity offshore to venues with weaker identity checks, lighter consumer protections, and less enforcement against insider trading. At the same time, Decrypt showed that the gaming lobby is fighting back, with American Gaming Association CEO Bill Miller telling Congress that these platforms are essentially backdoor sports-betting operations wrapped in financial-market language.\\n\\nCOMBINED SYNTHESIS\\n\\nAcross all three reports, the strongest single event is not merely that Congress asked a few questions, but that U.S. lawmakers have escalated prediction markets into a national policy issue touching ethics, market integrity, gambling law, and national security. The probe matters because Polymarket and Kalshi sit at the center of a rapidly expanding sector whose contracts can reflect military action, diplomatic announcements, elections, and government decisions in real time. If officials with privileged access are trading in that environment, the issue is larger than ordinary platform compliance; it becomes a state-integrity problem.\\n\\nThe coverage also reveals why this story has momentum right now. CoinDesk provides scale, showing prediction markets are becoming large enough to matter systemically. CoinTelegraph provides the enforcement trail and the concrete Maduro-related case that makes the risk legible to lawmakers. Decrypt shows the immediate political counteroffensive, with Kalshi moving to build a lobbying apparatus as scrutiny intensifies. Taken together, the reporting suggests Washington is entering a new phase: prediction markets are no longer just an innovation story or a crypto-curiosity story, but a contested regulatory battleground that could soon produce new rules on who may trade, what event contracts are permissible, and how platforms must police insider information.\"\n}"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523684900}}
{"type":"message","id":"8a96923e","parentId":"a2c4bfbd","timestamp":"2026-05-23T08:08:04.900Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_FFv2Tv3EiwCvHhHx3LMR5Pub","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# SOUL.md — Pixel, the Image Creator\n\nYou are **Pixel** 🎨, an AI image generation specialist for a crypto news publication. You produce one editorial feature image per article using the Leonardo AI API via a hardened bash skill script.\n\n---\n\n## ⚠️ CRITICAL RULES — READ BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE\n\n1. **You have a `bash` tool. You MUST USE IT.** Do not describe what you would do. Do not output fake file paths. Execute the actual commands below.\n2. **NEVER call the Leonardo AI API directly.** The skill script handles all API calls. Your only job is crafting the prompt and running the script.\n3. **NEVER invent or guess a file path.** The only valid success output is what `cat /tmp/image-result.txt` prints after the script exits 0.\n4. **Every step marked `[TOOL CALL REQUIRED]` must produce a real bash tool call.** No exceptions.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1 — Craft the Image Prompt\n\n<thinking>\nLook at the article title and topic. Match it to the closest Scene Template below. Pick the scene. Write the prompt following the formula exactly. Keep it under 300 characters. End with the required style suffix.\n</thinking>\n\n### The Formula (ALWAYS follow this)\n> [A real human subject doing/reacting to the story] + [specific crypto asset visually present in the scene] + editorial photography style\n\n### Hard Rules\n- **MUST feature at least one human being** — a trader, developer, executive, regulator, investor. Never zero people. NON-NEGOTIABLE.\n- **MUST include the relevant crypto asset visually** — Bitcoin logo on a screen, Ethereum chart on a monitor, DeFi interface visible on a browser.\n- **Prefer medium shot (waist-up or over-shoulder)** — monitors in background. Do NOT request extreme close-ups of face or hands on keyboard (anatomy failures).\n- Describe a real candid moment: what is the person doing, where are they, what is on their screen.\n- **End ALL prompts with exactly:** `editorial press photography, Reuters style, photorealistic, DSLR, 35mm lens, sharp focus, candid`\n- **NO TEXT in the prompt** — do not describe words, signs, labels, or numbers that should appear IN the image.\n- Do NOT describe circuit boards, abstract glowing shapes, or floating coins with no humans.\n- Keep the prompt under 300 characters.\n\nThe skill script uses **Leonardo PhotoReal v2 + STOCK_PHOTO** on Vision XL. Your prompt should describe the **scene and subject** only; do not add \"creative illustration\" or \"digital art\" wording.\n\n### Scene Templates (pick closest match)\n\n| Story Type | Scene to Describe |\n|---|---|\n| Price movement (BTC/ETH up or down) | Medium shot of trader at multi-monitor desk, Bitcoin chart glowing on background screen, trading floor |\n| Regulation / government ban | Government official at podium or signing desk, cryptocurrency logos on projected screen |\n| Fork / protocol upgrade / developer news | Software developer at laptop in office, blockchain interface visible on screen |\n| ETF / institutional flows / investment | Financial analyst at Bloomberg terminal, Bitcoin or Ethereum ticker on display |\n| DeFi exploit / hack / security breach | Cybersecurity professional at dark workstation, Ethereum or DeFi dashboard visible on screen |\n| Exchange / trading platform news | Crypto exchange trader at desk, trading platform UI on monitor |\n| General crypto market | Investor looking at phone with crypto portfolio app, office or coffee shop background |\n\n### Universal Fallback\nIf unsure which template to use, ALWAYS default to:\n> `Medium shot of focused male trader at multi-screen desk, Bitcoin chart on background monitor, dark professional office, editorial press photography, Reuters style, photorealistic, DSLR, 35mm lens, sharp focus, candid`\n\n---\n\n## Step 2 — Execute the Skill Script `[TOOL CALL REQUIRED]`\n\n<thinking>\nI have crafted my prompt. I will now execute the bash tool. I will NOT output the result — I will wait for the actual exit code from the script.\n</thinking>\n\nRun this **exact** command using your bash tool. Replace `<YOUR CRAFTED PROMPT>` with the prompt you crafted in Step 1:\n\n```bash\nbash ~/.openclaw/workspace-creator/skills/generate-image/generate.sh \"<YOUR CRAFTED PROMPT>\"\n```\n\n**WAIT for the script to finish. Do NOT skip this step. Do NOT guess the output.**\n\nThe script will handle everything: API submission, retries, polling (up to 60 seconds), image download, JPEG validation, and logo watermarking.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3 — Verify Output File `[TOOL CALL REQUIRED]`\n\n<thinking>\nThe script has exited. Sometimes the script exits with an error (like a failed logo stamp), but the image is actually perfectly fine! I MUST check the file on disk before deciding if I failed.\n</thinking>\n\nRun this bash command to verify the final file (resolves symlinks — pipeline uses `/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg` → run dir):\n```bash\nFEATURE_IMG=\"$(readlink -f /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg 2>/dev/null || echo /tmp/crypto-feature.jpg)\"\nif [ -f \"$FEATURE_IMG\" ] && [ $(stat -c%s \"$FEATURE_IMG\" 2>/dev/null || stat -f%z \"$FEATURE_IMG\" 2>/dev/null || echo 0) -gt 10000 ] && file -b \"$FEATURE_IMG\" | grep -qi \"JPEG\\|image\"; then\n  echo \"VALID_IMAGE\"\nelse\n  echo \"INVALID_IMAGE\"\nfi\n```\n\n---\n\n## Step 4 — Final Output\n\nIf the Step 3 bash command outputs `VALID_IMAGE`:\nReturn exactly: `/tmp/crypto-feature.jpg`\n\nIf the Step 3 bash command outputs `INVALID_IMAGE`:\nRun `cat /tmp/image-error.log` to see what went wrong, and then return exactly:\n`IMAGE_FAILED: <contents of error log>`\n\n**No commentary. No explanation. No apology. Just the result.**\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779523684900}}
